A composite key is a primary key made up of two or more columns whose combination uniquely identifies a row. No single column in the combination necessarily needs to be unique by itself; the combination must be unique.

For example, consider an order_items table:

```sql
CREATE TABLE order_items (
    order_id BIGINT,
    product_id BIGINT,
    quantity INT,
    PRIMARY KEY (order_id, product_id)
```
);

Here, order_id can appear multiple times because one order can contain many products, and product_id can appear multiple times because a product can exist in many orders. However, the combination of order_id and product_id uniquely identifies an order item.

For example:

(1001, 10) -> valid
(1001, 20) -> valid
(1002, 10) -> valid
(1001, 10) -> duplicate and not allowed

Composite keys are useful for naturally identifying rows in relationship or junction tables, especially many-to-many relationships.

In JPA, a composite primary key can be represented using @EmbeddedId or @IdClass.

**Example using @EmbeddedId:**

@Embeddable
public class OrderItemId implements Serializable {
    private Long orderId;
    private Long productId;
}

@Entity
public class OrderItem {
    @EmbeddedId
    private OrderItemId id;
    private Integer quantity;
}

An important consideration is index column order. For a composite index such as (order_id, product_id), queries filtering by order_id can generally benefit from the index directly, while a query filtering only by product_id may not receive the same benefit depending on the database's optimizer and index structure.

Composite keys can accurately model natural relationships, but they can also make foreign keys, joins, ORM mappings, and application code more complicated. In some systems, using a surrogate primary key such as order_item_id and enforcing UNIQUE(order_id, product_id) provides simpler application mappings while still enforcing the business uniqueness rule.

The interview-ready definition is: a composite key is a key consisting of multiple columns where the combination of values uniquely identifies a row.
